---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-ireland`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in Ireland',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitamin D deficiency', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in Ireland', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in Ireland: One of Europe's Cloudiest, Highest-Latitude Countries"
  description="At 51–55°N with famously grey skies, Ireland has a long vitamin D winter. FSAI guidance, the all-ages supplement advice, infant drops, and why the cloud makes it worse."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-ireland", label: "Vitamin D in Ireland" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in Ireland</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Ireland is a near-perfect recipe for low vitamin D: a latitude of 51–55°N, a maritime climate
      that keeps the skies overcast for much of the year, and a population that is largely fair-skinned
      but spends the winter indoors. The sun sits too low from roughly late October to March to drive
      any skin synthesis at all — and even in summer, the famous cloud cover steals a chunk of the UVB
      that does arrive.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">How widespread is deficiency?</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Irish studies consistently find high rates of low vitamin D, especially in winter and spring and
      among older adults. The National Adult Nutrition Survey and TILDA (the Irish Longitudinal Study on
      Ageing) have reported that a large minority of older Irish adults are deficient, with rates
      climbing further in those who are housebound. Deficiency is also common in people with darker skin
      and in pregnancy.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Official guidance</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The Food Safety Authority of Ireland (FSAI) has moved toward broad, all-ages supplement advice
      because of how reliably levels fall in the Irish winter. Recommendations include a routine
      <strong>daily supplement for infants</strong> (around 5 µg / 200 IU in the first year in older
      guidance, with more recent advice specifying continued supplementation) and a winter supplement
      of the order of <strong>10–15 µg (400–600 IU) per day</strong> for older children and adults,
      with the FSAI's more recent framework recommending year-round or extended supplementation for many
      groups. Public messaging encourages fortified foods and supplements rather than relying on sun.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why the cloud matters</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Latitude sets the ceiling on how much UVB is even possible; cloud then removes a further share.
      Heavy overcast can cut UVB by half or more, so an Irish summer delivers less synthesis than the
      same latitude would in a drier, sunnier climate. This is why Irish guidance leans harder on
      supplements than some continental neighbours. See our pages on
      <a href="/vitamin-d-and-cloud-cover" class="text-amber-700 underline">cloud cover</a> and
      <a href="/vitamin-d-by-latitude" class="text-amber-700 underline">latitude</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      For most people in Ireland, a daily vitamin D supplement in the 10–15 µg (400–600 IU) range
      through the winter half of the year — and often year-round for at-risk groups — is a sensible
      default, with infant drops as a routine measure and testing reserved for those with symptoms or
      risk factors.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Guidance reflects FSAI/HSE public advice for a general
      audience. Check specifics with your GP or pharmacist, especially for infants, pregnancy, and
      anyone with a medical condition.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-ireland" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
